\subsection{Random-Subset Ablation Details}
\label{sec:random_subset_ablation}

To evaluate whether the ablation effect in \S\ref{sec:learned_routing} is specific to instruction-dominant heads, we compare dominant-head ablation against 10 random subsets of 24/32 L1 heads (each subset drawn without replacement).
Dominant-head ablation increases action $R^2$ by $+0.042$ (within-experiment baseline $0.517$), while random subsets yield a mean increase of $+0.077 \pm 0.013$.
The dominant-head effect falls below the random distribution ($z = -2.73$, $p = 0.003$), indicating that instruction-dominant heads contribute to both routing and action encoding.

